CURRENT EE GDB STATUS (November 2026)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
GNU GDB 17 (mips64r5900el-ps2-elf-gdb) has been validated against ps2gdb
on real PS2 hardware through PS2Link over Ethernet. The following operations
were exercised: attach, read PC/SP/LO/HI, write registers, read/write stack
memory, source breakpoints, instruction stepping, backtraces, detach, and
reconnection after forcefully terminating an attached debugger.

PS2DEV already installs GDB. It is not bundled with Binutils; it is a separate
GNU debugger. ps2gdb provides the EE-side remote stub that communicates with
GDB over TCP port 12. The sample requires ps2ips.irx and a live PS2Link host:
file service. No IOP reset is performed inside the example program.

Installing the public EE API:
  make install

Installs libps2gdbStub.a and ps2gdb.h under $(PS2SDK)/ports/lib and
$(PS2SDK)/ports/include. A staged installation is supported with:
  make -C ee install PS2GDB_INSTALL_PREFIX=/tmp/ps2gdb-test-install

The command script is ps2.gdb (GDB commands, not a shell script). Update its
remote IP before using 'mips64r5900el-ps2-elf-gdb -x ps2.gdb'.

Building and running the example:
  make clean all check
  cd sample
  ps2client -h <PS2-IP> -t 120 -e PS2GDB_SAMPLE_DONE execee host:testgdb.elf

Leave ps2client running to serve host: files and console logging. In another
terminal, from the repository root:
  mips64r5900el-ps2-elf-gdb sample/testgdb.elf
  (gdb) target remote <PS2-IP>:12
  (gdb) break testgdb.c:13
  (gdb) continue
  (gdb) print i
  (gdb) set variable i = 100
  (gdb) stepi
  (gdb) detach

The test program ends in SleepThread(), not process exit. After detaching,
run 'ps2client -h <PS2-IP> reset' in a new host terminal to restore PS2Link.
Do NOT call SifIopReset or exit() inside the EE test program.

Compatibility and limitations:
  * GNU GDB's built-in mips:5900 target represents the low 64 bits of
    the EE's 128-bit GPR/HI/LO registers. The stub preserves the high 64
    bits when the debugger edits those registers but does not yet expose
    their values through a custom GDB target description.
  * Only one EE execution context is exposed to GDB. IOP debugging, full
    EE thread enumeration, asynchronous Ctrl-C while executing, and hardware
    watchpoints are not implemented here.
  * The memory service currently restricts remote accesses to safe
    ordinary EE RAM; arbitrary MMIO and privileged memory require a
    separate debugger design. Software breakpoints are supported via
    GDB's memory-write fallback.
  * The legacy PS2IPS/PS2Link network setup is still a prerequisite.

To verify the host-side wire format with an installed EE GDB without a PS2:
  make check
  ./tests/mock_rsp_server 21599 &
  mips64r5900el-ps2-elf-gdb -nx -q sample/testgdb.elf \
    -ex 'target remote 127.0.0.1:21599' \
    -ex 'info registers pc sp' -ex detach -ex quit

This repository's license contains academic/non-commercial conditions;
review it before reuse in a differently licensed project.

2024 Current State by crt0

Writing/Reading variables, memory, registers (only as 32bit), call stack - works.

Example usage: run sample with ps2link:
ps2client -h 192.168.1.13 execee host:testgdb.elf
in nanother terminal
mips64r5900el-ps2-elf-gdb -x ps2.gdb

